<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George Martinuzzi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1482">1482</a>–<a href="/years/1551">1551</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as George Utješenović, Frater Georgius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">George Martinuzzi was a Croatian-born Pauline monk and statesman who served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-zapolya">John Zápolya</a> as treasurer and became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Várad. After Zápolya's death in 1540 he governed the eastern Hungarian lands for the infant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-sigismund-zapolya">John Sigismund</a>, balancing between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. In 1551 he arranged the transfer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> to Ferdinand I and was made a cardinal, but was murdered that December by Habsburg officers who suspected him of dealings with the Ottomans.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-martinuzzi.jpg" alt="George Martinuzzi" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fr%C3%A1ter_Gy%C3%B6rgy_MTA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
